WEBVTT

00:00:06.542 --> 00:00:10.250
<v><b>Interviewer:</b></v> Do you think children should be made to study classic literature at school?

00:00:12.584 --> 00:00:14.579
<v><b>Emma:</b></v> I don’t know about the classic literature bit.

00:00:14.644 --> 00:00:18.068
I kind of think it’s been similar for a long time, hasn’t it,

00:00:18.093 --> 00:00:20.285
the kind of texts that you have to study at school?

00:00:20.412 --> 00:00:23.614
I think it’s far more important to get children into reading.

00:00:23.770 --> 00:00:29.351
And I think there’s such a diverse range out there now of, you know,

00:00:29.376 --> 00:00:32.535
young adult books and all kinds of things that children might be interested in

00:00:32.693 --> 00:00:36.563
that I think is more relative to kind of what they’re going through

00:00:36.563 --> 00:00:41.877
and might inspire them to get reading and then maybe progress to some classics if they wanted to.

00:00:41.973 --> 00:00:45.812
<v><b>Joanne:</b></v> So perhaps the more important thing is actually just to get children reading,

00:00:45.899 --> 00:00:48.909
whatever type of literature that, that takes.

00:00:48.910 --> 00:00:49.538
<v><b>Emma:</b></v> Yeah.

00:00:49.664 --> 00:00:50.976
<v><b>Joanne:</b></v> Rather than what it is that they read.

00:00:51.008 --> 00:00:53.523
I mean, arguably, if the child is given Shakespeare,

00:00:53.523 --> 00:00:57.107
Shakespeare can be really hard to access, can’t it?

00:00:57.107 --> 00:01:01.096
Really hard to read, and a child who doesn’t find it easy to read actually just might find

00:01:01.096 --> 00:01:03.741
that really difficult, it might just put them off reading.

00:01:03.742 --> 00:01:08.087
<v><b>Sean:</b></v> I think that’s the important thing; it’s all about when it’s introduced. I remember…

00:01:08.112 --> 00:01:08.954
<v><b>Joanne:</b></v> Yes, and how, actually.

00:01:08.954 --> 00:01:10.795
<v><b>Sean:</b></v> Yeah, and how, yeah, definitely. I think,

00:01:11.469 --> 00:01:17.246
I remember being a 14, 15-year-old in class reading Shakespeare and…

00:01:17.271 --> 00:01:18.820
<v><b>Joanne:</b></v> Thinking ‘What?’

00:01:18.845 --> 00:01:20.418
<v><b>Sean:</b></v> Why on earth are we doing this?

00:01:20.418 --> 00:01:23.232
Yeah, I couldn’t think of anything more useless, I think,

00:01:23.232 --> 00:01:28.149
and then I remember a turning point at about 16, thinking, hang on,

00:01:28.149 --> 00:01:29.707
this is actually quite interesting.

00:01:29.707 --> 00:01:34.053
You know, this is someone who wrote something 500 years ago

00:01:34.078 --> 00:01:36.269
and I’m sort of getting what they mean.

00:01:36.300 --> 00:01:40.678
And I think there was something really quite magical in that, that there was that continuity.

00:01:40.766 --> 00:01:42.278
<v><b>Joanne:</b></v> And maybe if you hadn’t been made to read that,

00:01:42.303 --> 00:01:45.215
you’d have probably never have picked that book up off the shelves.

00:01:45.215 --> 00:01:47.830
<v><b>Sean:</b></v> Yeah, I think that’s, that’s the thing; if you don’t do it at school,

00:01:47.855 --> 00:01:50.467
when will you see the classics? Yeah.

00:01:51.030 --> 00:01:53.471
<v><b>Emma:</b></v> And I guess it’s how it’s taught to you as well.

00:01:53.511 --> 00:01:57.188
If you see it as kind of a boring text that doesn’t mean anything to you,

00:01:57.188 --> 00:02:02.559
but then, with Shakespeare, for example, all the idioms and all the language that he created,

00:02:02.559 --> 00:02:05.602
that makes it more related to everyday life, doesn’t it?

00:02:05.673 --> 00:02:06.302
<v><b>Joanne:</b></v> Yes.

00:02:06.421 --> 00:02:10.677
<v><b>Emma:</b></v> But then, I think, some classics, I read my first Jane Austen recently,

00:02:10.725 --> 00:02:15.104
and just the dialogue, and it’s quite long-winded,

00:02:15.144 --> 00:02:20.094
and they’re all obsessed with marriage and stuff, and that just kind of…turns you off, doesn’t it?

00:02:20.284 --> 00:02:23.587
Um, so I think, it depends on the books.

00:02:24.212 --> 00:02:28.091
<v><b>Joanne:</b></v> Yes, I think it does. And, and how they’re put across, I think, is really important, too.

00:02:28.116 --> 00:02:32.699
And maybe classical, but also being part of a much wider curriculum.

00:05:35.131 --> 00:05:37.131
<v><b>Emma:</b></v> Yeah, definitely.